  "result": "Before the city wakes, it belongs to the people who open things: bakers lifting shutters, drivers unfolding bus routes, cleaners turning keys in doors that everyone else will later pass through without noticing. Dawn is less a spectacle than a handover, conducted between those finishing work and those beginning it.\n\nA bakery window glows against the blue street. Inside, someone tips flour onto a wooden table, raising a weather system. There is something reassuring about this ordinary cloud. Not every important event announces itself with a headline. Some arrive as warm bread wrapped in paper, carried home beneath an arm.\n\nAt the bus stop, two strangers stand close enough to share the shelter but far enough apart to preserve their private worlds. One checks the time. The other watches a pigeon inspect a bottle cap. For a moment, the pigeon seems to have the more interesting appointment of the three.\n\nSoon the traffic will thicken. Messages will multiply, coffee will cool beside keyboards, and the day will acquire its familiar urgency. But right now, a woman outside the flower shop is trimming stems over a bucket. She pauses to straighten one yellow tulip, then turns it toward the empty pavement.\n\nThe bus arrives with a sigh of brakes. Both strangers step aboard, and the pigeon retreats beneath the bench. Through the bakery door comes a man carrying two paper bags. He stops at the flower shop, shifts breakfast into one hand, and points at the tulip with the other, smiling.",
